On 2008-03-12, Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> The problem here is that you're thinking like an insider. 
>
> I *am* an insider. As a newbie I wouldn't be able to judge at all. :-}

You mean "not judge accurately".  Newbies definitely form opinions.

>> Think instead
>> like a player, who says "Hey, what the hell?  I'm fire resistant, why is
>> the fire elemental burning me?  The red dragon couldn't breathe on me...
>> WTF?  This game has bugs, it sucks."
>
> Sure, that's most likely possible. 

It's actually almost guaranteed.  Has to do with how common "fire
resistance" and "fire elementals" are across many gaming worlds, and how
consistent those definitions are.  Fire elementals _always_ do fire
damage, and fire resistance _always_ protects from fire damage.

Now, if you're in a world where fire resistance doesn't ever provide
100% protection, then people aren't surprised when fire elementals hit
lots harder than anything else they've run into before.  But given that
we're in a world that _does_, to have fire elementals suddenly break
that is extremely jarring and really highlights the inconsistency.

Elaborate rationalizations aren't going to bring the player back at that
point; he's always going to think that the designer was a little off the
wheels when he made that stuff.

> example about such deliberate design decisions. BTW, another one
> that may astonish a newbie is that the E word doesn't work for every
> creature. "Hey, what the hell?  I've burned the word..." - you see?)

Difference here: the function of Elbereth scaring creatures is not
something a new player expects, and indeed many new players need to have
it pointed out to them (having not read the Guidebook, because that's
documentation and gamers don't read-before-playing).  In fact, I'm
pretty sure the whole idea of "I write this world on the ground and all
the monsters run away" is unique to Nethack, so there is no weight of
ages here pressing in a specific direction.

>> Sometimes the big picture has to outweigh the details, even if there's a
>> perfectly valid way to rationalize the decision.  This is one of those cases.
>>
>> I would rather not add features that require elaborate rationalizations
>> to justify -- especially when the inconsistency would be so glaring as
>> "fire elementals aren't affected by 100% fire resistance".
>
> Here we apparently disagree. Magic (as most of Nethack) is hardly
> explainable in a rational way, and Nethack's fire is just partly
> "physical" (traps, potions of oil, lamps, yes; dragon breath(?),
> wands(??), spells(???), hmm...).

Unfortunately, you're fighting against standard fantasy magic here, not
just Nethack's magic.  Everything has to have a minimum of consistency,
or we might as well have fire elementals doing ice damage.

> I seem to recall that you (or was it David?) once said that the
> gameplay is the primary motivation for the design of features. All
> the rationalizations (equivalence to Real Life or artificial ones)
> help to make Nethack not too surprising. The "fire magic" is not a
> critical type of rationalization. (IMO.)

I did indeed say that.  However, slavish adherence to _any_ principle
sets the stage for an elaborate failure -- and making fire elementals do
damage to people who are 100% fire resistant, _while every other form of
fire in the game doesn't_, would be that failure.  It is simply going
too much against the "stream" of player expectations that have been set
by prior games _and_ have been set by the game itself up to that point;
again, trying to argue that red dragon breath doesn't burn you at 100%
resistance but fire elementals do?  Flimsy ground.

